# CQ4b-tolerant · For which specimens do two datings disagree, once the
#                 source's OWN stated precision is applied?
#
# Expected: fewer rows than cq04b.rq.
#
# Decision D7. The strict query compares calibrated intervals as if their
# endpoints were facts. They are not: Schwarz et al. state that "the precision
# of the radiocarbon dates of medieval samples are often better than ±25 to 30
# years for the 1 sigma statistical uncertainty of the calibrated age
# interval". The ±30 below is THEIR figure, not ours — which is what makes it
# usable. Inventing a tolerance would be the reduction this project argues
# against, committed by us.
#
# Run both. Where the two queries differ, the disagreement was an artefact of
# reading a hedged estimate as a boundary.
PREFIX po: <https://w3id.org/paleont/>

SELECT DISTINCT ?specimen ?datingA ?datingB
WHERE {
  ?datingA a po:DatingClaim ; po:aboutSpecimen ?specimen .
  ?datingB a po:DatingClaim ; po:aboutSpecimen ?specimen .
  FILTER(STR(?datingA) < STR(?datingB))
  FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?datingA po:isNegated true }
  FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?datingB po:isNegated true }

  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    ?datingA po:hasResult ?ia . ?ia po:startYear ?sa ; po:endYear ?ea .
    ?datingB po:hasResult ?ib . ?ib po:startYear ?sb ; po:endYear ?eb .
    FILTER(?ea + 30 >= ?sb && ?eb + 30 >= ?sa)
  }
}
